{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Last month, I stepped off the Shinkansen in Kyoto and into a world that felt both ancient and alive. Japan didn’t just welcome me—it whispered to me.\n\nI wandered through Fushimi Inari at dawn, the vermilion torii gates forming a tunnel of quiet reverence. No crowds, just the rustle of wind and the distant chime of a bell. In Gion, I caught a glimpse of a geisha gliding down an alley, her obi a splash of silk against weathered wood. I didn’t snap a photo—I let her vanish into history.\n\nIn Osaka, I devoured takoyaki from a street vendor who grinned as he flipped the batter with a wooden paddle. “One more?” he asked. I nodded. The hot, savory bites melted on my tongue—simple, perfect.\n\nTokyo was a symphony of contrasts: neon signs blaring over temple roofs, salarymen bowing deeply in the subway, and a vending machine on every corner offering hot coffee, cold beer, or even umbrellas in a downpour. I spent an afternoon in the Robot Restaurant (yes, it was as surreal as it sounds), then found peace hours later in the moss garden of Ryoan-ji, staring at fifteen stones and wondering which ones I was meant to see.\n\nAt a ryokan in Hakone, I soaked in an onsen under a sky dusted with stars, the steam curling around me like a secret. My host, an elderly woman with kind eyes, served kaiseki—a meal that felt like poetry on porcelain. Each dish told a story: the season, the mountain, the sea.\n\nI left Japan with more than souvenirs. I left with silence in my bones—the kind you only find when you stop chasing and start noticing.\n\nJapan doesn’t demand your attention. It waits. And when you finally listen, it gives you everything.\n\n— 297 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "Last", "logprob": -1.208087682723999}, {"token": " month", "logprob": -0.016866806894540787}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -5.602820692729438e-06}, {"token": " I", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token